WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.946
Rust's Result and Option types encourage
explicit handling of failure and absence.

2
00:00:04.026 --> 00:00:06.550
That is a security advantage when teams
use it intentionally.

3
00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.687
Instead of letting unexpected states drift
silently through the program,

4
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:13.262
Rust makes many failure paths visible in
function signatures and compiler feedback.

5
00:00:13.342 --> 00:00:15.720
The value comes from designing those paths
deliberately.

6
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.003
Careless use of unwrap or expect can turn
predictable failure into unexpected

7
00:00:19.083 --> 00:00:20.491
termination or fragile behavior.

8
00:00:20.571 --> 00:00:23.192
Panics may be acceptable in some startup
validation, test code,

9
00:00:23.272 --> 00:00:26.184
or invariant-checking situations, but
production request handling,

10
00:00:26.264 --> 00:00:27.866
background jobs, message processors,

11
00:00:27.946 --> 00:00:30.275
and long-running services need clear
failure behavior.

12
00:00:30.355 --> 00:00:32.248
A malformed request, missing optional
value,

13
00:00:32.328 --> 00:00:35.968
or unavailable dependency should not
automatically become a service-wide reliability

14
00:00:36.048 --> 00:00:36.437
problem.

15
00:00:36.517 --> 00:00:39.283
Error messages should be useful without
exposing secrets, tokens,

16
00:00:39.363 --> 00:00:42.227
sensitive configuration, internal
topology, private identifiers,

17
00:00:42.307 --> 00:00:43.860
or excessive implementation detail.

18
00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.609
Internal logs may need more context than
user-facing responses,

19
00:00:46.689 --> 00:00:48.825
but even internal logs should avoid
sensitive data.

20
00:00:48.905 --> 00:00:52.157
Error handling should support diagnosis
without spreading secrets or turning

21
00:00:52.237 --> 00:00:54.178
implementation details into public
behavior.

22
00:00:54.258 --> 00:00:57.267
Secure applications define which failures
should be retried, rejected,

23
00:00:57.347 --> 00:00:59.143
logged, surfaced to the caller, escalated,

24
00:00:59.223 --> 00:01:01.067
or treated as startup-blocking conditions.

25
00:01:01.147 --> 00:01:04.399
They also avoid retry behavior that
creates overload and avoid silent fallback

26
00:01:04.479 --> 00:01:05.838
behavior that weakens security.

27
00:01:05.918 --> 00:01:07.908
Fail-safe Rust code is not code that never
fails;

28
00:01:07.988 --> 00:01:10.900
it is code that fails in controlled,
observable, and appropriate ways.
